diagnostics/traits.em:33:10: `greet` takes different parameters from the `greet` of `Named` it overrides
      func greet(other: String): Int {
           ^^^^^
An override takes exactly the parameters it replaces, with the same names and types, since a call through `Named` passes them: `()`.
diagnostics/traits.em:33:32: `greet` gives Int, but the `greet` of `Named` it overrides gives String
      func greet(other: String): Int {
                                 ^^^
An override gives what it replaces, or a subclass of that class. Write `: String`.
diagnostics/traits.em:42:10: `greet` is a method of the trait `Named`
      func greet(): String {
           ^^^^^
Write `@override` on the line before it to supply `Named`'s method, or give this one a name of its own.
diagnostics/traits.em:51:7: `Named` and `Loud` both have `name`, but they do not agree on it
  class Both with Named, Loud {
        ^^^^
`Both` can adopt both only if their `name` has the same kind and type. Rename it in one of them.
diagnostics/traits.em:51:7: `Both` gets `introduction` from both `Named` and `Loud`, and trait order does not choose
  class Both with Named, Loud {
        ^^^^
Give `Both` its own `introduction` marked `@override`. It can run either version, as in `Named.introduction(self)`.
diagnostics/traits.em:68:16: Point is not a trait, so it cannot follow `with`
  class Pet with Point {
                 ^^^^^
Only a trait, declared with `trait`, can be adopted with `with`.
