Name a different trait after `with`, or remove it.
diagnostics/traits.em:82:19: `Named` is a trait, so it cannot be constructed
      const other = Named()
                    ^^^^^
A trait is a contract. Construct a struct or class that adopts `Named` instead.
diagnostics/traits.em:83:11: values seen through a trait cannot be compared yet
      print(named == named)
            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
A struct compares by its fields and an object by identity, and a trait may hold either. Compare the concrete values instead.
diagnostics/traits.em:84:17: `_prefix` is private to `Named`
      print(named._prefix())
                  ^^^^^^^
Only code written inside `Named`'s braces can reach a name that starts with `_`.
diagnostics/traits.em:85:5: `named` is a parameter, so a change to it would be lost when the function returns
      named.nickname = "z"
      ^^^^^
Copy it into a `var`, change the copy, and return it, as in `var changed = named`.
diagnostics/traits.em:88:11: `Named.greet` has no default to run
  const c = Named.greet(Forgot())
            ^^^^^^^^^^^
`greet` is a requirement, with no body in the trait. Call it on a value instead, as in `value.greet()`.
